WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.900
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:07.200
The Roman Colosseum is one of the Earth’s
greatest monuments to humanity’s long and

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.730
rugged history.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.120
From Verona to Pompeii and even in Africa,
amphitheatres were erected by the Roman Empire

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.630
to entertain the public in the most inhumane
ways possible.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:24.130
Ferocious beasts such as lions, tigers, elephants
and bears would have to fight for their lives

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.900
against gladiators, who were usually slaves
and criminals.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.899
Sometimes the animals would be made to fight
each other and of course there were the public

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:38.910
executions, a spectacle where criminals would
be tied up like meat and an array of wild

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.250
animals would feed upon them.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:47.760
Out of these horrors grew a great need for
captured wild beasts and so was born one of

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.809
the most dangerous jobs mankind has ever performed,
“The Beast Hunters”.

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:58.739
A group of pioneering entrepreneurs who risked
their lives to hunt down and capture wild

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:04.750
beasts then brought them back to amphitheatres
all over the empire for use in the games.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.320
Tigers came from Armenia.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.110
Elephants, giraffes, ostriches, leopards and
hippos from Africa.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.280
And bears came from Northern Europe.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:20.390
Records show of a man who specialised in the
capture of wild bears whose name was “Ursarius”

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.479
literally translated as “bear-hunter”.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.630
The hunters couldn’t use bows or spears
because of the risk of inflicting a wound

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.430
on the animal that would become infected.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.680
The animal needed to be in good health, ready
to fight its opponents when it arrived at

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.729
the amphitheatre.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:41.950
Tranquiliser darts didn’t exist then so
these beast hunters had to be creative to

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.009
catch their unfortunate prey.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:49.880
One popular method was to dig a large pit
in the ground and stick a wooden stake in

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.079
the center of the pit with a dead lamb on
top.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:58.920
The hunters would then hide and patiently
wait for a lion or other beast to come and

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.110
investigate the bait.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.670
As soon as the lion jumps in the pit the hunter
would lower a cage from above, trapping it.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:11.009
Another, more obnoxious method was for several
hunters to ride horses around the animal whilst

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.390
banging on drums.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:19.690
The creature would chase the various horses
and eventually fall over due to exhaustion,

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.970
and so it could be safely captured.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:27.250
Of course when dealing with such dangerous
predators in close confines, that haven’t

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:33.980
been sedated, accidents do happen and many
a beast hunter lost their life whilst trying

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.760
to catch lions and bears or whilst transporting
them back to Italy.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:44.989
Surely workplaces have become more regulated
now since the days of the Roman Empire?

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:47.480
Deaths must be far less common?

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:57.560
Well that’s true except for a few extremely
high-risk jobs, one of which is one of the

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.610
deadliest jobs in the World today…

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.689
Welcome to the treacherous ship yards of Chittagong.

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:10.351
It’s a hive of industry and it generates
massive wealth for a few rich families, but

00:03:10.351 --> 00:03:14.520
its also one of the most dangerous places
to earn a living in the world.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.049
It’s a dark industry called “ship breaking”.

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:23.659
When their time roaming the seas is over,
ships from all over the world, oil tankers,

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:29.709
cruise ships, cargo ships etc. are brought
to the very tip of the Bay of Bengal, to their

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:34.290
final resting place at Chittagong, Bangladesh.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:39.129
This is where ships come to die and so do
people.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:45.180
Ships have a typical lifespan of 25-30 years
before corrosion takes over and makes maintenance

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.190
too difficult or expensive.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:53.890
Now considering each ship costs roughly $1
billion to build, and they have to be replaced

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:59.730
every 25 years, that makes it an expensive
and very lucrative business.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.439
Of course, when there’s hundreds of millions
of dollar’s worth of valuable steel and

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:09.349
other metals within the vessel’s framework
you don’t just want to let it sink to the

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.420
bottom of the ocean and slowly degrade.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.620
So companies that own ships that have reached
the end of their useful life, send the dying

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:25.460
vessel off to a ship-breaking yard where it
is painstakingly dismantled, entirely by hand.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:30.560
The salvaged raw materials, usually hundreds
of thousands of tons worth, are then sent

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.000
back to the owner of the ship so they can
sell it to all sorts of different construction

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.590
and recycling companies, making back a portion
of the cost of building the ship in the first

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.590
place.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:45.020
And the ship breaking yard is paid for their
services.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.669
That all sounds very well.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:53.620
But the process of dismantling a vessel so
huge and dealing with such large, heavy pieces

00:04:53.620 --> 00:05:02.340
of metals at great heights on a daily basis
is not only extremely perilous but often life-threatening.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:09.350
In fact, ship breaking is so darn dangerous
that the whole industry was banned entirely

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.660
in the Western world by the 1980s.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:18.039
Of course that left a huge gap in the market,
a gap worth billions.

00:05:18.039 --> 00:05:23.340
One country where it still isn’t banned
to this day came flying into the market and

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:29.540
thus become one of the world’s largest players
in the ship breaking industry, Bangladesh.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.910
Since then other Eastern countries have bitten
off a chunk of the market, such as India,

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.020
China, Pakistan and Turkey.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:43.360
Today India is the world’s largest ship
breaker, followed closely by Bangladesh.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.229
Yet despite the competition, the city of Chittagong
in Indonesia is world famous for its ship

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:53.569
breaking yard, but for all the wrong reasons.

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:59.370
It’s one of the most un-regulated and dangerous
ship-breaking yards out there.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:04.970
Along with the deadly Alang ship breaking
yard in India, which happens to be the world’s

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:05.970
largest.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:10.860
But why is ship breaking so unforgivingly
dangerous?

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.520
One of the biggest threats is falling metal.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.849
Huge metal sheets are constantly breaking
off and tumbling down the sides of these gigantic

00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:22.740
metal structures, many workers are killed
after being hit in the head or completely

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.539
crushed by pieces of falling metal.

00:06:25.539 --> 00:06:31.240
There’s also a major risk of asbestos, lead
or heavy metals poisoning.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:37.080
Asbestos in particular is an extremely common
construction material in older vessels and

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:43.110
workers commonly develop fatal cancers and
other illnesses due to the many toxins they’re

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.509
exposed to on a daily basis.

00:06:45.509 --> 00:06:50.569
Some ship components are filled with deadly
gasses that can escape when dismantled, instantly

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:56.550
poisoning or suffocating all surrounding workers,
this has happened on multiple occasions.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:03.039
And then there are the fires and explosions,
using power tools and blowtorches on a vessel

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:09.479
that likely contains a myriad of flammable
fuels, gasses and materials causes frequent

00:07:09.479 --> 00:07:12.900
fires and sometimes devastating explosions.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:19.729
If all that isn’t bad enough, many workers
are killed by simply plummeting to their death

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:23.080
after taking a wrong step atop of the vessel.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:30.990
And for risking their lives, ship breaking
yard workers are paid a measly $3 per day.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:35.180
But the West is not without its perilous professions.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:41.199
One of the deadliest jobs you can do is lumberjacking
also known as logging.

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:46.740
America was built by lumberjacks, they felled
the trees that created the first houses and

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.990
the first towns.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:54.069
Despite all the technology available to us,
the humble art of felling trees is still a

00:07:54.069 --> 00:07:57.789
commonly practiced profession, especially
in North America.

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:04.759
However, in a recent study it was revealed
that lumberjacking or logging has the most

00:08:04.759 --> 00:08:12.270
injuries and fatalities per 100,000 workers
than any other profession, except one, but

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.690
I’ll talk about that in a minute.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.540
It was followed closely by fishermen.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:24.270
Loggers are 40 times more likely to die at
work than any other profession.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:29.629
So what makes chopping down a bunch of trees
so damn dangerous?

00:08:29.629 --> 00:08:35.170
Well a good place to start is the 100 metre
plus, tall trees that are plummeting down

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:37.780
right next to you several times per hour.

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:42.190
All it takes is the slightest error in the
incision for the tree to fall in the wrong

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:48.890
direction, trees can easily kickback, launching
themselves right at you and barks can split

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.800
and splinter which can be deadly.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:57.460
84% of deaths in logging are due to falling
trees or rolling logs.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:02.010
Surprisingly only 16% are due to machinery.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:07.780
Yet when chainsaw accidents do happen they
are often gruesome and fatal.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:13.170
This x-ray of a tree-surgeon whose chainsaw
slipped and went into his chest tells a thousand

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.170
words.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:19.890
But things have definitely improved from the
pioneering days of the early logging industry.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:25.200
Before the widespread of electricity, sawmills
were powered by steam which meant they needed

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.510
a constant supply of water.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:32.450
Thus they were often positioned down river
whereas trees would be felled further up the

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:33.640
river.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.520
There were no vehicles up to this job, so
how do you transport extremely heavy and cumbersome

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.650
logs to the sawmill, for processing into more
manageable planks?

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:49.250
Why send them floating down the river of course.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:54.220
Ingenious yet simple, this was known as “log
driving”.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:00.100
But logs would often get stuck causing a large
pile-up, so specialist water runners known

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:06.130
as “water rats” would rapidly hop from
one slippery, wet, rolling log to another

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:11.880
and hunt for the log causing the jam so they
could free it up.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:16.520
As you can imagine fatalities were frequent
among these “water rats”.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:22.810
The current is ferociously strong and all
it takes is one wrong step to be swept under.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:31.550
Whilst all of these jobs are inherently dangerous,
non more so than the statistically most dangerous

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.510
job in the world.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:37.970
With more deaths per head than any other profession
on Earth.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:44.380
What if I told you that there’s a job that
has an 18% fatality rate, compared to loggers

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:50.970
with a fatality rate of 0.13% and fishermen
with a rate of 0.12%?

00:10:50.970 --> 00:11:01.400
That job is president of the United States,
out of the 44 previous presidents 8 have died

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:06.540
whilst in office, a staggering fatality rate
of 18%.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:12.560
Four of whom were assassinated, meaning 9%
of US presidents have been killed whilst in

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:13.560
office.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:19.740
And some people spend millions of dollars
and dedicate their lives to achieve this prestigious

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.250
yet frightfully dangerous job.

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:30.340
Proving once and for all that the bigger they
come, the harder they fall.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:40.240
Thanks for watching.

